WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:02.520
We're at a tipping point in human history,

00:02.520 --> 00:05.960
a species poised between gaining the stars

00:05.960 --> 00:08.440
and losing the planet we call home.

00:08.440 --> 00:10.080
Even in just the past few years,

00:10.080 --> 00:13.280
we've greatly expanded our knowledge of how Earth fits

00:13.280 --> 00:15.680
within the context of our universe.

00:15.680 --> 00:18.480
NASA's Kepler mission has discovered thousands

00:18.480 --> 00:21.000
of potential planets around other stars,

00:21.000 --> 00:23.280
indicating that Earth is but one of billions

00:23.280 --> 00:25.600
of planets in our galaxy.

00:25.600 --> 00:28.040
Kepler's a space telescope that measures

00:28.040 --> 00:31.440
the subtle dimming of stars as planets pass in front of them,

00:31.440 --> 00:34.720
blocking just a little bit of that light from reaching us.

00:34.720 --> 00:37.320
Kepler's data reveals planets sizes,

00:37.320 --> 00:40.000
as well as their distance from their parent star.

00:40.000 --> 00:41.600
Together, this helps us understand

00:41.600 --> 00:44.080
whether these planets are small and rocky,

00:44.080 --> 00:46.800
like the terrestrial planets in our own solar system,

00:46.800 --> 00:50.080
and also how much light they receive from their parent sun.

00:50.080 --> 00:53.080
In turn, this provides clues as to whether these planets

00:53.080 --> 00:56.680
that we discover might be habitable or not.

00:56.680 --> 00:59.680
Unfortunately, at the same time as we're discovering

00:59.680 --> 01:02.720
this treasure trove of potentially habitable worlds,

01:02.720 --> 01:06.000
our own planet is sagging under the weight of humanity.

01:06.000 --> 01:10.320
2014 was the hottest year on record.

01:10.320 --> 01:13.760
Glaciers and sea ice that have been with us for millennia

01:13.760 --> 01:17.200
are now disappearing in a matter of decades.

01:17.200 --> 01:19.840
These planetary-scale environmental changes

01:19.840 --> 01:21.840
that we have set in motion

01:21.840 --> 01:26.200
are rapidly outpacing our ability to alter their course.

01:26.200 --> 01:29.920
But I'm not a climate scientist. I'm an astronomer.

01:29.920 --> 01:33.440
I study planetary habitability as influenced by stars

01:33.440 --> 01:35.680
with the hopes of finding the places in the universe

01:35.680 --> 01:38.800
where we might discover life beyond our own planet.

01:38.800 --> 01:42.800
You could say that I look for choice-alien real estate.

01:42.800 --> 01:45.000
Now, as somebody who is deeply embedded

01:45.000 --> 01:47.120
in the search for life in the universe,

01:47.120 --> 01:50.720
I can tell you that the more you look for planets like Earth,

01:50.720 --> 01:54.200
the more you appreciate our own planet itself.

01:54.200 --> 01:56.880
Each one of these new worlds invites a comparison

01:56.880 --> 02:00.480
between the newly discovered planet and the planets we know best,

02:00.480 --> 02:02.520
those of our own solar system.

02:02.520 --> 02:04.480
Consider our neighbour, Mars.

02:04.480 --> 02:08.240
Mars is small and rocky, and though it's a bit far from the sun,

02:08.240 --> 02:10.760
it might be considered a potentially habitable world

02:10.760 --> 02:12.880
if found by a mission like Kepler.

02:12.880 --> 02:16.320
Indeed, it's possible that Mars was habitable in the past.

02:16.320 --> 02:19.600
And in part, this is why we study Mars so much.

02:19.600 --> 02:22.920
Our rovers, like Curiosity, crawl across its surface,

02:22.920 --> 02:26.360
scratching for clues as to the origins of life as we know it.

02:26.360 --> 02:29.680
Orbiters like the MAVEN mission sample the Martian atmosphere,

02:29.680 --> 02:33.600
trying to understand how Mars might have lost its past habitability.

02:33.600 --> 02:36.000
Private space flight companies now offer

02:36.000 --> 02:37.960
not just a short trip to near space,

02:37.960 --> 02:42.160
but the tantalizing possibility of living our lives on Mars.

02:42.160 --> 02:46.200
But though these Martian vistas resemble the deserts of our own home world,

02:46.200 --> 02:48.240
places that are tied in our imagination

02:48.240 --> 02:52.040
to ideas about pioneering and frontiers,

02:52.040 --> 02:56.840
compared to Earth, Mars is a pretty terrible place to live.

02:56.840 --> 03:00.040
Consider the extent to which we have not colonized

03:00.040 --> 03:01.920
the deserts of our own planet,

03:01.920 --> 03:04.880
places that are lush by comparison with Mars.

03:04.880 --> 03:07.720
Even in the driest, highest places on Earth,

03:07.720 --> 03:10.120
the air is sweet and thick with oxygen,

03:10.120 --> 03:14.840
exhaled from thousands of miles away by our rainforests.

03:14.840 --> 03:15.840
I worry.

03:15.840 --> 03:20.800
I worry that this excitement about colonizing Mars and other planets

03:20.800 --> 03:23.280
carries with it a long, dark shadow,

03:23.280 --> 03:25.560
the implication and belief by some

03:25.560 --> 03:28.960
that Mars will be there to save us from the self-inflicted destruction

03:28.960 --> 03:33.120
of the only truly habitable planet we know of, the Earth.

03:33.120 --> 03:35.400
As much as I love interplanetary exploration,

03:35.400 --> 03:37.800
I deeply disagree with this idea.

03:37.800 --> 03:40.440
There are many excellent reasons to go to Mars,

03:40.440 --> 03:44.040
but for anyone to tell you that Mars will be there to back up humanity

03:44.040 --> 03:45.680
is like the captain of the Titanic

03:45.680 --> 03:49.000
telling you that the real party is happening later on the lifeboats.

03:49.000 --> 03:51.000
LAUGHTER

03:51.000 --> 03:55.000
APPLAUSE

03:55.000 --> 03:56.000
Thank you.

03:56.000 --> 03:58.000
APPLAUSE

03:58.000 --> 04:02.080
But the goals of interplanetary exploration and planetary preservation

04:02.080 --> 04:03.840
are not opposed to one another.

04:03.840 --> 04:06.640
No, they're in fact two sides of the same goal,

04:06.640 --> 04:10.800
to understand, preserve and improve life into the future.

04:10.800 --> 04:14.880
The extreme environments of our own world are alien vistas.

04:14.880 --> 04:16.840
They're just closer to home.

04:16.840 --> 04:21.240
If we can understand how to create and maintain habitable spaces

04:21.240 --> 04:24.520
out of hostile, inhospitable spaces here on Earth,

04:24.520 --> 04:27.760
perhaps we can meet the needs of both preserving our own environment

04:27.760 --> 04:30.000
and moving beyond it.

04:30.000 --> 04:34.120
I leave you with a final thought experiment, Fermi's Paradox.

04:34.120 --> 04:38.080
Many years ago, the physicist Enrico Fermi asked that,

04:38.080 --> 04:41.760
given the fact that our universe has been around for a very long time

04:41.760 --> 04:44.560
and we expect that there are many planets within it,

04:44.560 --> 04:47.760
we should have found evidence for alien life by now.

04:47.760 --> 04:49.560
So where are they?

04:49.560 --> 04:53.000
Well, one possible solution to Fermi's Paradox

04:53.000 --> 04:56.520
is that as civilizations become technologically advanced enough

04:56.520 --> 04:58.920
to consider living amongst the stars,

04:58.920 --> 05:01.400
they lose sight of how important it is

05:01.400 --> 05:06.160
to safeguard the home worlds that fostered that advancement to begin with.

05:06.160 --> 05:07.840
It is hubris to believe

05:07.840 --> 05:11.960
that interplanetary colonization alone will save us from ourselves,

05:11.960 --> 05:15.560
but planetary preservation and interplanetary exploration

05:15.560 --> 05:17.520
can work together.

05:17.520 --> 05:19.560
If we truly believe in our ability

05:19.560 --> 05:23.160
to bend the hostile environments of Mars for human habitation,

05:23.160 --> 05:26.120
then we should be able to surmount the far easier task

05:26.120 --> 05:28.720
of preserving the habitability of the Earth.

05:28.720 --> 05:30.000
Thank you.

